# Diagram rules

What makes an architecture diagram read well to a person, as rules a layout can be scored against.
`@dxos/diagram` loads this file (`Rules.RULES`); `Appeal` measures every `code` and `both` rule from the
scene's geometry, and the image judge asks every `vision` and `both` rule of a decision model shown
the rendered page. `weight` ranks the rules by the strength of the evidence behind them: crossings
first in every study that measured them, then orthogonality and flow, path continuity, bends, and
only then alignment, symmetry and compactness, which people prefer but trade against the rest.

Rules about what a diagram says (a title, a legend, labels naming technology, one level of
abstraction) are not here: they belong to `Architecture.RULES`, which judges content, and a layout
engine cannot change them.

Evidence keys used in `Source:` lines:

- Purchase 1997: "Which aesthetic has the greatest effect on human understanding?", GD'97, LNCS 1353.
- Purchase 2002: Purchase, Carrington & Allder, "Empirical evaluation of aesthetics-based graph layout",
  Empirical Software Engineering 7(3), and "Graph layout aesthetics in UML diagrams", JGAA 6(3).
- Ware 2002: Ware, Purchase, Colpoys & McGill, "Cognitive measurements of graph aesthetics",
  Information Visualization 1.
- HOLA: Kieffer, Dwyer, Marriott & Wybrow, "HOLA: Human-like orthogonal network layout", IEEE TVCG
  22(1), 2016 (InfoVis 2015).
- Ware 2020: Colin Ware, *Information Visualization: Perception for Design*, 4th ed., ch. 6 (Gestalt).
- Imhof 1975: "Positioning names on maps", The American Cartographer 2(2).
- C4: Simon Brown, the C4 model notation and diagram review checklist, https://c4model.com.
- Tufte: *The Visual Display of Quantitative Information* (data-ink) and *Envisioning Information*.
- Bertin: *Semiology of Graphics* (visual variables).

## Extensions

| Term           | URI                           |
| -------------- | ----------------------------- |
| `diagram-rule` | org.dxos.mdl.diagram-rule@1.0 |

## Crossings and edges

```mdl
diagram-rule no-edge-crossings: Arrows do not cross each other
  category: crossings
  evaluator: both
  weight: 10
  question: Can every arrow be followed without it crossing another arrow?
  yes: No two arrows cross, or one crossing is unavoidable and clearly at a right angle.
  no: Several arrows cross each other.
  The most damaging aesthetic in every study that measured it: it slows tracing and causes misreads.
  Code: the number of properly crossing connector pairs, scored 1 / (1 + crossings / 2).
  Source: Purchase 1997; Purchase 2002; Ware 2002; HOLA.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule no-edge-through-node: Arrows never pass through a box they do not connect
  category: edges
  evaluator: code
  weight: 8
  An arrow running across a box reads as a connection to it ("edge tunnelling").
  Code: 1 when no connector has an interior run through a box other than a frame, else 0.
  Source: Dunne et al. 2015, IBM J. R&D 59; libavoid (Wybrow, Marriott & Stuckey 2010).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule no-shared-trunks: Distinct arrows never run along the same line
  category: edges
  evaluator: both
  weight: 6
  question: Does every arrow have its own line, with no two arrows merged into one shared trunk?
  yes: Each arrow runs on its own line; parallel arrows keep a visible gap.
  no: Two or more arrows share a segment, so their ends cannot be paired up.
  Overlapping segments make it impossible to tell which arrow goes where; routers nudge parallel runs
  apart for this reason. A deliberate merge of same-meaning arrows into one target (an inheritance
  bus) does not count.
  Code: connector pairs that share a collinear run, scored 1 / (1 + pairs).
  Source: Wybrow et al. 2010 (nudging); Purchase 2002 ("no adjacent arrows" preferred).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule edges-head-toward-target: Each arrow takes a direct route to its target
  category: edges
  evaluator: both
  weight: 5
  question: Does each arrow run fairly directly from its source to its target, without long detours or doubling back?
  yes: Arrows take short, direct routes.
  no: Some arrows loop around the page, leave in the wrong direction, or double back.
  Readers predict where an arrow ends from the way it leaves its source, and path length is the
  strongest predictor of tracing time.
  Code: per connector, route length over the gap between its two boxes (at least 64 units); the share
  of connectors within twice that, softened by the mean excess. Measured against the boxes rather than
  the route's own ends, so a loop round the page and a pair of related boxes placed far apart both count.
  Source: Huang, Eades & Hong 2009 (geodesic-path tendency); Ware 2002.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule few-bends: Each arrow bends no more than its ends require
  category: edges
  evaluator: code
  weight: 4
  Bends rank below crossings, orthogonality and flow, but each one costs tracing time. Zero bends
  when the boxes line up, one for an L, two for a Z.
  Code: mean bends per connector beyond the minimum its end points allow, scored 1 / (1 + excess).
  Source: Purchase 1997; Purchase 2002; Tamassia 1987.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule straight-when-aligned: Boxes that face each other are joined by a straight arrow
  category: edges
  evaluator: code
  weight: 3
  When two connected boxes overlap across the flow, the arrow between them should be one straight
  segment: the cheapest bend saved, and collinearity aids recall.
  Code: among connectors whose end boxes overlap horizontally or vertically, the share drawn with no bend.
  Source: ELK Brandes-Köpf straightening; Marriott et al. 2012 (collinearity aids recall).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule uniform-edge-length: Arrows have similar lengths
  category: edges
  evaluator: code
  weight: 2
  Human-drawn layouts have uniform edge lengths, and their spread correlates with dislike.
  Code: 1 − min(1, coefficient of variation of connector lengths).
  Source: HOLA (R8); Dwyer et al. 2009; Purchase, Pilcher & Plimmer 2012.
```

## Flow

```mdl
diagram-rule consistent-flow: Most arrows run the same way
  category: flow
  evaluator: both
  weight: 5
  question: Do nearly all arrows run in one main direction (down, or left to right), with only a few pointing back?
  yes: The arrows share one direction; a reader follows dependencies one way.
  no: Arrows run in mixed directions, or whole chains point back against the flow.
  Information flow ranks third for software diagrams, after crossings and orthogonality. Arrows with
  a hollow triangle head point to an abstraction above by convention and do not count against it.
  Code: the share of connectors whose net displacement runs with the diagram's dominant direction.
  Source: Purchase 2002; Sugiyama, Tagawa & Toda 1981.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule continuous-paths: A chain through a box continues on its far side
  category: flow
  evaluator: code
  weight: 2
  A box with one arrow in and one out reads as a step on a path when the arrow leaves on the side
  opposite the one it entered by; turning back on the same side breaks the line of reading.
  Code: for boxes with one incoming and one outgoing connector, 1 for opposite sides, 0.5 adjacent, 0 same.
  Source: Ware 2002 (continuity); HOLA (R2).
```

## Alignment

```mdl
diagram-rule grid-alignment: Boxes line up in rows and columns
  category: alignment
  evaluator: both
  weight: 3
  question: Do the boxes line up in clear rows and columns?
  yes: Most boxes share a row or column with two or more others.
  no: Boxes are staggered, with few clear rows or columns.
  People prefer grid-like placement and choose it when they draw by hand.
  Code: the share of boxes whose centre lines up with at least two others horizontally or vertically.
  Source: HOLA (R4); Purchase, Pilcher & Plimmer 2012.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule no-near-misses: Boxes are either aligned or clearly apart
  category: alignment
  evaluator: code
  weight: 2
  Two boxes almost in line look like a mistake; it is the most visible sign of an automatic layout.
  Code: box pairs whose centres or edges differ by more than 1 and less than 12 units along an axis,
  scored 1 / (1 + pairs / 2).
  Source: HOLA (the gap between human and algorithmic layouts).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule symmetry: Symmetric structure is drawn symmetrically
  category: alignment
  evaluator: vision
  weight: 1
  question: Where the structure is symmetric, such as a fan-out to similar parts, is it drawn mirror-symmetrically?
  yes: Fan-outs and parallel paths are drawn evenly about an axis.
  no: Parts that play the same role are drawn lopsided or scattered.
  Symmetry aids understanding, recall and preference, though less than crossings.
  Source: Purchase 1997; Marriott et al. 2012; HOLA (R5).
```

## Groups

```mdl
diagram-rule frames-nested: Group frames are either nested or apart, never partly overlapping
  category: groups
  evaluator: code
  weight: 8
  Enclosure is the strongest grouping cue; a frame that partly overlaps another states no hierarchy.
  Code: 1 when every pair of frames is disjoint or one contains the other, else 0.
  Source: Palmer 1992 (common region); Ware 2020.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule frames-aligned: Groups line up with each other
  category: groups
  evaluator: both
  weight: 3
  question: Do the group frames line up with one another, sharing a top or left edge where they sit side by side or stacked?
  yes: Groups share edges and read as one arrangement.
  no: A group floats at an arbitrary height or offset beside another.
  Aligned bands read as layers; ragged frames look accidental.
  Code: the share of frames that share a top, bottom, left or right edge with another frame.
  Source: HOLA (gridiness); Tufte, *Envisioning Information* (layering and separation).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule frames-hug-members: Group frames fit their contents
  category: groups
  evaluator: code
  weight: 2
  A frame much larger than what it holds suggests missing members and wastes space.
  Code: per frame, the area of its members' bounding box over the frame's area, averaged and scaled
  so that 0.5 or more scores 1.
  Source: HOLA (R3 compactness); ELK padding.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule few-frame-crossings: Arrows cross group borders no more than they must
  category: groups
  evaluator: code
  weight: 3
  Every border crossing is noise, and an arrow that leaves and re-enters a group misstates membership.
  An arrow should cross only the borders between its two ends' groups.
  Code: border crossings beyond the minimum the group tree requires, scored 1 / (1 + extra / 2).
  Source: Eades, Feng & Lin 1996 (clustered graphs); Ware 2020 (connectedness and enclosure).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule gestalt-cues-agree: Each box's nearest neighbour is in the same group
  category: groups
  evaluator: code
  weight: 3
  When a box sits closer to a box in another group than to any in its own, proximity contradicts the
  frame and readers misread the grouping.
  Code: the share of boxes whose nearest other box shares their innermost frame.
  Source: Ware 2020 ch. 6; Palmer & Rock 1994.
```

## Space

```mdl
diagram-rule compact-drawing: The drawing uses its area well
  category: space
  evaluator: both
  weight: 3
  question: Is the drawing compact, with no large empty regions between or inside groups?
  yes: The page is evenly filled; connected boxes are near each other.
  no: There are large empty regions, stretched groups, or connected boxes far apart.
  Compactness correlates with preference, and spread lengthens arrows.
  Code: box area over the drawing's area, scored against a target of 0.25 (1 at or above it).
  Source: HOLA (R3); Ware 2002.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule balanced-whitespace: Ink is spread evenly across the page
  category: space
  evaluator: code
  weight: 2
  A crowded half next to an empty one looks unbalanced and suggests a grouping that is not there.
  Code: split the drawing into a 3 × 3 grid; 1 − min(1, CV / 1.5), with CV the coefficient of variation of box area per cell.
  Source: HOLA participant comments; Ngo, Teo & Byrne 2003 (balance).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule uniform-spacing: Gaps between neighbouring boxes are even
  category: space
  evaluator: code
  weight: 2
  Uneven gaps falsely imply grouping by proximity.
  Code: each box's gap to its nearest neighbour; 1 − min(1, coefficient of variation of those gaps).
  Source: ELK and Graphviz node spacing; Davidson & Harel 1996.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule landscape-aspect: The drawing is landscape and fits a screen
  category: space
  evaluator: code
  weight: 2
  A tall strip or a long ribbon has to be scaled down to fit a page, shrinking its text.
  Code: aspect ratio width / height scored 1 / (1 + |ln(ratio / 1.6)|).
  Source: ELK aspectRatio (1.6); Purchase 2002 (width of layout).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule readable-at-page-width: Text stays legible when the drawing fits a page
  category: labels
  evaluator: code
  weight: 3
  Diagrams are read as images in documents and pull requests; a drawing that must shrink below 11
  pixel text to fit 1200 pixels has failed however good its geometry.
  Code: the smallest text size after scaling the drawing to 1200 units wide, over 11, capped at 1.
  Source: C4 (diagrams should stand alone); Tufte (resolution).
```

## Labels

```mdl
diagram-rule labels-clear: Labels never overlap other labels, boxes or lines
  category: labels
  evaluator: both
  weight: 6
  question: Can every label be read on its own, without running into another label, a box or a line?
  yes: Every label sits clear of other text and lines.
  no: Some label overlaps another label, a box or a line, or is cut off.
  Overlapping text is illegible; label placement treats non-overlap as a hard constraint.
  Code: label overlaps counted by `Diagnostics` plus labels that overflow their boxes, scored 1 / (1 + count).
  Source: Imhof 1975; Kakoulis & Tollis 1997.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule labels-attached: Each arrow label sits nearest its own arrow
  category: labels
  evaluator: both
  weight: 3
  question: Is it clear which arrow each arrow label belongs to?
  yes: Every label sits on or right beside its own arrow.
  no: Some label sits between arrows, or nearer another arrow than its own.
  A label equally near two arrows is a misreading waiting to happen.
  Code: the share of arrow labels whose nearest connector is their own.
  Source: Imhof 1975; ELK edge-label placement.
```

## Overall

```mdl
diagram-rule consistent-notation: The same kind of thing always looks the same
  category: hierarchy
  evaluator: vision
  weight: 2
  question: Does each kind of element (box, group, arrow style) look the same everywhere, with no unexplained differences in size, colour or line style?
  yes: Every kind of element has one consistent look.
  no: Similar elements differ in size, colour or line style for no evident reason.
  Size, colour and line style are read as meaning; inconsistent use invites false distinctions.
  Source: Bertin; C4 checklist; Ware 2020 ch. 5.
```

```mdl
diagram-rule minimal-non-data-ink: Nothing is drawn that carries no information
  category: hierarchy
  evaluator: vision
  weight: 2
  question: Is the drawing free of decoration that carries no information, such as background grids, shadows, heavy borders or gradients, with group frames lighter than the boxes they hold?
  yes: Every mark carries meaning; frames recede behind the components.
  no: Background grids, heavy frames or decoration compete with the structure.
  Non-data ink competes with structure; frames are context, so they should recede.
  Source: Tufte (data-ink ratio, layering and separation).
```

```mdl
diagram-rule tidy-overall: The diagram looks deliberately drawn
  category: hierarchy
  evaluator: vision
  weight: 4
  question: Does this look like a tidy diagram a careful designer drew by hand, rather than an automatic layout?
  yes: It looks deliberate: aligned, balanced, uncluttered, easy to scan.
  no: It looks machine-made: ragged, cluttered, with odd gaps or detours.
  The gap between human-drawn and algorithmic layouts is the gap this library exists to close.
  Source: HOLA; Purchase, Pilcher & Plimmer 2012.
```

## Appendix

```mdl
ext diagram-rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.diagram-rule@1.0
  desc: A rule a diagram's drawing can be scored against.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    category: crossings | flow | alignment | space | groups | labels | edges | hierarchy
    evaluator: code | vision | both
    weight: Number
    question?: Prose
    yes?: Prose
    no?: Prose
```
